{
  "reservedCodePrefixAcrossUpgrades": {
    "_info": {
      "comment": "The reserved code prefix across the schedule, with its own control. Both payloads deploy a single byte of code: index 0 deploys 0xFE and index 1 deploys 0xEF. Through Magneto both are created; from Mystique EIP-3541 rejects the 0xEF one and still accepts the other. The prefix is the only difference between them, so the control removes gas, the initcode and the factory as explanations, and the rejection can only be the leading byte. A single byte of deployed code is deliberate: it is the shortest contract that can carry a prefix at all, so nothing about the body can be confused for the cause. Storage slot 0 holds the created address, zero when creation failed, and slot 1 is set to 1 unconditionally as a liveness marker -- without it a zero address and an unreached store are the same reading. The rule reserves 0xEF for a later object format, so what is asserted here is the reservation and not any behavior of that format, which this chain does not have.",
      "oracle": "core-geth production, evm t8n at each upgrade's rules, chain id 61",
      "oracle-version": "v1.12.21-unstable",
      "generated": "2026-08-23",
      "signing": "txbytes carries the signed transaction; the signing key is a filling input, not a fixture field, and the harness reads `sender`.",
      "control": "Data index 0 deploys 0xFE and must be created at every upgrade, including those where index 1 is rejected.",
      "unfilled-no-fork-name": "No expectation for ETC_Thanos: the generator's fork table has no name for them, so their rules cannot be selected. A tooling gap, not a claim that the case does not apply.",
      "unfilled-not-a-state-rule": "No expectation for ETC_DAOFork, ETC_DAOWars, ETC_FrontierThawing, ETC_MESSOff, ETC_MESSOn: these change no state-transition rule, so a state fixture has nothing to assert about them. Covering them needs block-level or chain-selection tests."
    },
